<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! IdentitySlot ~ a narrow door, three ways through

A vessel asks an identity provider for very little: a DID, a stable actor id, and a verdict on a
delegation. Bluesky, GitHub and Keyhive each satisfy that surface, and none of them appears in the
shape.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! The invariants

# ''The interface stays MINIMUM.'' It carries what a vessel and Automerge's share policy need, and
nothing more — so Keyhive's full three-layer stack slots in behind it without moving a signature.
# ''A derived actor id stays DETERMINISTIC.'' The same signing key yields the same actor across
reboots. A fresh id each boot would fork the document's own history against itself.
# ''Every method runs ASYNC.'' An implementation may reach IndexedDB in a browser or a key file on a
node, and a synchronous shape would have forced that work into construction.
# ''A capability token stays OPAQUE.'' A UCAN JWT with its proof chain, a Keyhive convergent
capability, or null where nothing gates — the shape carries the token and reads none of it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-quine-split>>

!! The quine split ~ class in code, CONFIG in the wiki

''Crypto primitives cannot live in the wiki'', so the implementation class stands in TypeScript. The
identity CONFIG stands in the wiki: which DID, which provider, which capability token, each as a
`lar:` tiddler an operator can read and override from inside the running wiki.

''`$:/` stays RESERVED for TW5 core and TW5 plugins.'' Every Lararium config takes a `lar:` URI, and
that choice does work beyond tidiness — a `lar:` tiddler stands as a heleuma sync candidate and a
`$:/` tiddler does not, so config written to the reserved space would never travel.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-the-open-slot-leaves>>

!! What the open slot leaves ungated, and what gates anyway

`OpenIdentitySlot` mints a placeholder DID and derives a stable actor id by digest over the vessel id.
''It names the USER-level layer, and that layer stands as an alpha stub.''

''The gates that DO stand sit elsewhere, and the asymmetry holds by design:''

* Keyhive runs LIVE at the OPERATOR level — the daemon's founding gates, the peer-boundary gate, the
binding mint.
* A node's share policy admits an inbound network peer only through the daemon-auth gate.
* A browser admits only same-origin and in-process peers, so it reaches no peer a node would have had
to authenticate.

Naming this slot as the house's access gate would promise a reader a check that stands one layer up.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

